<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<sdf version="1.9">
  <!--
    ============================================================================
    FALCON V2 - Gazebo Sim Harmonic aircraft model
    ============================================================================
    Coordinate frame: Gazebo/CAD FLU body frame throughout (+X forward,
    +Y left, +Z up). All 12 STL meshes share one common global/assembly
    coordinate system; no mesh was re-origined or re-centered. Raw mesh
    coordinates are used with only the STL_SCALE_TO_SI = 0.001 scale factor
    applied (millimetres to metres). No XFLR5-frame value is used anywhere
    in this file.

    ============================================================================
    MASS DISTRIBUTION
    ============================================================================
    Total mass is exactly 6.000 kg across all 8 links:
      - left_prop / right_prop: their real component mass, 0.0301 kg each.
        Each prop's ROTATIONAL INERTIA is tagged
        V1_PROVISIONAL_ROTATIONAL_INERTIA - exact prop rotational inertia is
        not known; see the per-link comments.
      - left_aileron / right_aileron / left_elevator / right_elevator /
        rudder: no component-level mass exists in the source data for these
        parts (airframe structural mass is an undifferentiated remainder).
        Each is assigned a TEMPORARY_NUMERICAL_MASS of 0.001 kg - a
        near-massless numerical minimum, NOT a physical claim.
      - base_link carries the remainder:
        6.000 - (2*0.0301 + 5*0.001) = 5.9348 kg.
    No known component mass is double-counted: motor mass (0.286 kg total)
    stays inside base_link, since the motors are fixed visuals rather than
    separate links; propeller mass (0.0602 kg total) is subtracted from
    base_link exactly once.

    ============================================================================
    COLLISION STRATEGY
    ============================================================================
    No raw high-poly STL mesh is used as collision geometry anywhere in this
    file. Primitives only:
      - Fuselage: 3 boxes along X (nose + wing-root band, mid-fuselage, tail
        band), sized from the confirmed bounding-box and slice data. The
        mid-fuselage box's Y half-width has no direct slice measurement in
        the source data and is conservatively set equal to the confirmed
        tail-band half-width (0.280 m) rather than interpolated narrower:
        tagged ASSUMPTION, deliberately oversized, never undersized.
      - Left/right wing: one box each, using each wing's own full mesh
        bounding box. This is a coarse, deliberately conservative (oversized
        at the tapered tip) V1 approximation, not a tapered or
        multi-segment fit.
      - Horizontal/vertical tail (fixed portions): no separate collision
        primitive. The fixed horizontal-stabiliser and vertical-fin
        structure is part of body.stl, and the fuselage tail-band box above
        already fully envelops that region (its Y = +/-0.280 m and
        Z = [0, 0.330480] m span exceed both the elevator's 240 mm half-span
        and the rudder's 299.75 mm max height). A second primitive there
        would duplicate collision volume.
      - Motors (left/right): one small box each, using the motor mesh's own
        bounding box. Carries no mass - SDF collision geometry is massless;
        mass is set only in <inertial>.
      - Movable control surfaces (aileron/elevator/rudder): each gets its own
        thin box collision matching its own mesh bounding box, expressed in
        the link's own local frame (offset from the link's hinge-point
        origin). Full derivation in the per-link comments below.
      - Propellers: no collision geometry. A rotating thin disk at high RPM
        has no ground-contact or self-collision use case here, and adding
        one risks spurious self-collision artifacts.

    self_collide is explicitly false at the model level: several
    movable-surface meshes intentionally abut or slightly overlap their
    parent structure at the hinge line (e.g. aileron vs. wing cutout), and
    enabling self-collision would produce spurious contact forces there.

    ============================================================================
    HINGE-AXIS FITTING METHOD (aileron / elevator / rudder)
    ============================================================================
    Each hinge axis is fitted by direct re-parse of the relevant STL file's
    own vertex data (read-only; no mesh file is modified), sliced into narrow
    +/-3 mm bands centred on each documented %chord station, with a
    deterministic tip-outlier exclusion. Per-surface fit residuals are
    reported in the per-link comments below.

    ============================================================================
    CONTROL-SURFACE JOINT LIMITS
    ============================================================================
    All 5 control-surface joints use <limit><lower>/<upper> of
    +/-0.7853981634 rad (+/-45 deg exactly, pi/4), tagged
    MECHANICAL_ACTUATOR_LIMIT_V1.

    TWO DISTINCT CONCEPTS - DO NOT CONFLATE:
      (1) MECHANICAL travel capability = +/-45 deg = +/-0.7853981634 rad,
          tag USER_CONFIRMED_MECHANICAL_CAPABILITY. This is what the <limit>
          element below encodes: what the servo/joint can physically be
          commanded to reach.
      (2) The AERODYNAMICALLY VALIDATED deflection range, which is a
          separate, narrower question and is not what <limit> encodes.

    ============================================================================
    PROPELLER LINKS/JOINTS
    ============================================================================
    Hub positions:
      left  hub = (0.2951, +0.3000, 0.1271) m
      right hub = (0.2951, -0.3000, 0.1271) m
    Axis: +X for both (measured prop-face normal
    (+0.999996, +0.000018, -0.002668), essentially pure +X, ~0.153 deg off).
    Joint type: revolute with a very large <limit> (+/-1e16 rad) to emulate
    continuous rotation - SDF has no dedicated "continuous" joint type.

    Rotation DIRECTION (left = CCW, right = CW) is recorded in comments only.
    It is NOT encoded by the joint definition: a single +X axis has no
    inherent CCW/CW sense until a plugin commands a signed velocity or
    torque.

    VIEWING-REFERENCE-FRAME CAVEAT: the source data states only the bare fact
    "Left prop = CCW / Right prop = CW" - it does NOT state a viewing
    reference frame (from behind vs. from the front), and no other source
    supplies one. CCW/CW is not physically meaningful without a stated
    viewpoint, since the same rotation looks CCW from one end of the shaft
    and CW from the other. This viewing-frame detail is therefore
    DATA_REQUIRED, not assumed. The left propeller's commanded velocity must
    be positive-CCW and the right positive-CW, but WHICH viewing convention
    those were measured against must be confirmed from the manufacturer
    manual or CAD source before any signed velocity command is written:
    SIGN_TEST_REQUIRED and VIEWPOINT_CONFIRMATION_REQUIRED, neither resolved
    here.

    VISUAL-ONLY propeller scale correction: scoped to <visual> only. The STL
    mesh propeller diameter of ~0.2734 m is VISUAL_MESH_ONLY and must NEVER
    be used by physics code; the real physical propeller for all propulsion
    calculations is the APC 13x6.5E at D = 0.3302 m. An additional 1.208x
    visual-only enlargement (0.001 * 1.208 = 0.001208) is applied to the
    propeller <visual><mesh><scale> so the model displays at a realistic
    size. Because a uniform scale multiplies all vertex coordinates about
    the mesh's own distant local origin, applying 0.001208 directly would
    also shift the rendered hub ~60 mm from its true position; to keep the
    enlarged disk centred on the real hub, the <visual><pose> translation is
    T = -1.208 * hub_position_m (verified numerically to reproduce the hub
    to better than 0.1 mm). No <collision> exists for the props, so this
    scale can never leak into a collision or physics computation.
    ============================================================================
  -->

  <model name="falcon_v2">
    <static>false</static>
    <self_collide>false</self_collide>
    <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>

    <!-- ============================================================
         BASE LINK: fixed structure - body, both wings, both motor
         nacelles (visual only), full aircraft mass minus the 7 movable
         child links, Gazebo/CAD CG, V1_PROVISIONAL whole-aircraft
         inertia.
         ============================================================ -->
    <link name="base_link">
      <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>

      <inertial>
        <!--
          CG: Gazebo/CAD reference CG (0.168309, 0, 0.100000) m - CONFIRMED.
          This is the ONLY CG used anywhere in this file; the XFLR5
          reference CG (0.0637, 0, -0.0210) is NEVER used for SDF purposes.
          Mass: 5.9348 kg = 6.000 kg total - 0.0652 kg on the 7 movable
          child links (see the mass-distribution header comment).
          Inertia: V1_PROVISIONAL whole-aircraft tensor, about the
          Gazebo/CAD CG.
          Ixz sign convention vs. SDF's expected off-diagonal sign is
          EXPLICITLY UNVERIFIED: entered here exactly as documented
          (+0.01485) and flagged rather than silently resolved.
        -->
        <pose>0.168309 0 0.100000 0 0 0</pose>
        <mass>5.9348</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.7284</ixx>
          <iyy>0.2507</iyy>
          <izz>0.9523</izz>
          <ixy>0</ixy>
          <ixz>0.01485</ixz>
          <iyz>0</iyz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>

      <!-- Fixed visuals: mesh in raw global/assembly coordinates,
           strategy (A): link origin = model origin = (0,0,0), only the
           STL_SCALE_TO_SI=0.001 scale applied, no additional visual pose
           offset, since the mesh already carries the correct absolute
           position for every fixed part. -->
      <visual name="body_visual">
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <mesh>
            <uri>meshes/body.stl</uri>
            <scale>0.001 0.001 0.001</scale>
          </mesh>
        </geometry>
      </visual>

      <visual name="left_wing_visual">
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <mesh>
            <uri>meshes/left_wing.stl</uri>
            <scale>0.001 0.001 0.001</scale>
          </mesh>
        </geometry>
      </visual>

      <visual name="right_wing_visual">
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <mesh>
            <uri>meshes/right_wing.stl</uri>
            <scale>0.001 0.001 0.001</scale>
          </mesh>
        </geometry>
      </visual>

      <!--
        Motor visuals: mesh kept at its own raw global coordinates, with no
        re-offset. The documented "motor center" reference is
        (0.2623, +/-0.3000, 0.1269) m, which the mesh's own authored
        placement is cross-checked against; the mesh itself is NOT
        re-translated to that point. It already sits in shared global
        coordinates matching that reference to within the documented ~7.3 mm
        bounding-box-vs-named-centre difference, which is deliberately not
        resolved by re-offsetting the mesh. The raw mesh, at scale 0.001 and
        zero additional pose, is what is rendered.
      -->
      <visual name="left_motor_visual">
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <mesh>
            <uri>meshes/left_motor.stl</uri>
            <scale>0.001 0.001 0.001</scale>
          </mesh>
        </geometry>
      </visual>

      <visual name="right_motor_visual">
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <mesh>
            <uri>meshes/right_motor.stl</uri>
            <scale>0.001 0.001 0.001</scale>
          </mesh>
        </geometry>
      </visual>

      <!-- Fixed collision: primitives only, see collision-strategy
           header comment for full reasoning and provenance of every
           box. -->

      <!-- Fuselage box 1: nose + wing-root band. X in
           [-0.027512,0.526880] (nose tip 0.526880 m confirmed
           GEOMETRY.md section 13/14; -0.027512 m = wing mesh's own
           forward X bound). Y=+/-0.094649 m = CONFIRMED wing-root-station
           body slice (GEOMETRY.md section 25). Z=[0,0.330480] m = full
           confirmed body Z bound (no nose-specific Z slice exists; using
           the full range is a deliberately conservative/oversized
           choice, not a measured nose cross-section). -->
      <collision name="fuselage_collision_fwd">
        <pose>0.249684 0 0.165240 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.554392 0.189298 0.330480</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
      </collision>

      <!-- Fuselage box 2: mid-fuselage, between the wing-root band and
           the tail band. X in [-0.472684,-0.027512] m (spans between the
           wing mesh's forward bound and the elevator/rudder mesh's
           aft-most X bound). Y=+/-0.280000 m is ASSUMPTION: no confirmed
           mid-fuselage slice exists in the source of truth; the
           confirmed tail-band half-width (0.280 m, wider than the
           confirmed wing-root half-width of 0.094649 m) is used here as
           a deliberately conservative (oversized, never undersized)
           stand-in. Z full body range as above. -->
      <collision name="fuselage_collision_mid">
        <pose>-0.250098 0 0.165240 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.445172 0.560000 0.330480</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
      </collision>

      <!-- Fuselage box 3: tail band. X in [-0.526880,-0.472684] m (tail
           tip to the elevator/rudder mesh's own forward X bound).
           Y=+/-0.280000 m = CONFIRMED tail-station body slice
           (GEOMETRY.md section 25): this same box also serves as the
           horizontal/vertical-tail fixed-structure collision volume, per
           the collision-strategy header comment (its Y and Z extents
           exceed both the elevator half-span and the rudder height). Z
           full body range, CONFIRMED (body's own global max Z occurs in
           this X-band, GEOMETRY.md section 14.1). -->
      <collision name="fuselage_collision_tail">
        <pose>-0.499782 0 0.165240 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.054196 0.560000 0.330480</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
      </collision>

      <!-- Left/right wing collision: single box per wing, each wing's
           own full mesh bounding box (GEOMETRY.md section 13/15).
           Deliberately coarse V1 approximation: a constant-chord box is
           oversized outboard relative to the real tapered planform (root
           chord 0.260 m vs. tip chord 0.051 m); this is a conservative
           simplification, not a tapered/multi-segment fit. -->
      <collision name="left_wing_collision">
        <pose>0.105743 0.566014 0.127019 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.266510 0.972027 0.046956</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
      </collision>

      <collision name="right_wing_collision">
        <pose>0.105743 -0.566014 0.127019 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.266510 0.972027 0.046956</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
      </collision>

      <!-- Motor collision: small box per motor nacelle, each motor's own
           full mesh bounding box (GEOMETRY.md section 13/19). Not
           explicitly required by the task's named collision list; added
           as a low-cost completeness extension (carries no mass). -->
      <collision name="left_motor_collision">
        <pose>0.269577 0.299975 0.126974 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.066484 0.038225 0.038237</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
      </collision>

      <collision name="right_motor_collision">
        <pose>0.269577 -0.299975 0.126974 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.066484 0.038225 0.038237</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
      </collision>

      <!-- ============================================================
           SENSOR SUITE V1. Native gz-sim System-plugin-backed sensors
           (IMU/GPS/baro) - the system plugins that process these <sensor>
           elements are loaded at the WORLD level, not here.

           MOUNT LOCATION (all sensors, including the custom pitot and
           magnetometer plugins below): co-located with the Gazebo/CAD CG at
           (0.168309, 0, 0.100000) m - the same point already authoritative
           for this link's <inertial><pose> above. This is a documented
           choice, not an arbitrary number: the real physical mounting
           location of any avionics sensor on the airframe is DATA_REQUIRED
           (no CAD or manual placement data exists), so this model uses the
           one documented, authoritative point on the airframe rather than
           inventing an unsourced offset. That also avoids introducing an
           unvalidated lever-arm velocity effect (V_point = V_cg + omega x r)
           that no real data could confirm. Tag: SIMULATION_ASSUMPTION.

           NOISE: off by default in every sensor below (zero-noise
           deterministic mode) - no <noise> sub-element is present. An
           optional V1_PROVISIONAL noise block is shown commented out under
           each sensor for future use.

           UPDATE RATES: none are airframe- or autopilot-specific datasheet
           values (none exist in the source data). Each is a documented,
           order-of-magnitude-reasonable V1_PROVISIONAL choice.
           ============================================================ -->

      <!-- IMU: orientation_reference_frame=CUSTOM, parent_frame="world",
           custom_rpy="0 0 0" - not the "ENU" localization string, and not
           the boot-relative CUSTOM-with-empty-parent_frame default. This is
           deliberate for clarity rather than because "ENU" was found broken:
           the imu.sdf spec documents parent_frame="world",
           custom_rpy="0 0 0" as an explicit, unambiguous alternative ("IMU
           reports in Gazebo world frame") that avoids depending on the
           ENU string's own indirection through SphericalCoordinates.

           The reported orientation quaternion is the rotation of body FLU
           relative to the Gazebo world frame (ENU) - the direct input to
           plugins/sensors/Frames.hh's AttitudeFluEnuToFrdNed() for
           conversion to FRD/NED. Angular velocity and linear acceleration
           (specific force) are reported in the sensor's own frame, which is
           body FLU: this sensor has zero relative rotation to base_link. -->
      <sensor name="imu_sensor" type="imu">
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>100</update_rate>
        <topic>/model/falcon_v2/sensors/imu</topic>
        <pose>0.168309 0 0.100000 0 0 0</pose>
        <imu>
          <orientation_reference_frame>
            <localization>CUSTOM</localization>
            <custom_rpy parent_frame="world">0 0 0</custom_rpy>
          </orientation_reference_frame>
        </imu>
        <!-- V1_PROVISIONAL_SENSOR_NOISE example (OFF - not applied unless
             uncommented and stddev values are sourced/updated):
        <imu>
          <angular_velocity>
            <x><noise type="gaussian"><mean>0</mean><stddev>0.001</stddev></noise></x>
            <y><noise type="gaussian"><mean>0</mean><stddev>0.001</stddev></noise></y>
            <z><noise type="gaussian"><mean>0</mean><stddev>0.001</stddev></noise></z>
          </angular_velocity>
          <linear_acceleration>
            <x><noise type="gaussian"><mean>0</mean><stddev>0.02</stddev></noise></x>
            <y><noise type="gaussian"><mean>0</mean><stddev>0.02</stddev></noise></y>
            <z><noise type="gaussian"><mean>0</mean><stddev>0.02</stddev></noise></z>
          </linear_acceleration>
        </imu>
        -->
      </sensor>

      <!-- ============================================================
           ARDUPILOT-BRIDGE IMU RELAY SENSOR (additive; imu_sensor above is
           NOT modified).

           WHY A SECOND IMU SENSOR INSTANCE: the ardupilot_gazebo
           ArduPilotPlugin does not read a sensor's own SDF <topic> value.
           It independently computes its subscribe topic as
           gz::sim::scopedName(imuEntity, ecm) + "/imu", i.e. the gz-sim
           auto-generated default sensor topic
           "/world/<world>/model/falcon_v2/link/base_link/sensor/<name>/imu".
           imu_sensor above declares a CUSTOM <topic>
           (/model/falcon_v2/sensors/imu), and a gz-sensors sensor with an
           explicit <topic> publishes ONLY on that custom topic, never
           additionally on the auto-generated default. ArduPilotPlugin's
           computed topic would therefore never match, imuMsgValid would
           never become true, and CreateStateJSON() would silently never
           build a packet - the bridge would structurally never send any FDM
           data to ArduPlane, rather than merely reporting wrong numbers.

           This second, ArduPilot-bridge-only sensor instance is added
           deliberately WITHOUT a <topic> element, so gz-sim's Sensors system
           assigns it exactly the auto-generated default topic that
           ArduPilotPlugin expects. Mount point, orientation_reference_frame,
           update rate and zero-noise mode are identical to imu_sensor above
           - this is a transport-layer duplicate for ArduPilot's exclusive
           use, not a second physical sensor model - EXCEPT for its <pose>
           ROTATION component; see the FLU->FRD comment immediately below. -->

      <!-- ============================================================
           FLU->FRD GYRO/ACCEL CORRECTION.

           ArduPilotPlugin::CreateStateJSON() writes this sensor's raw
           imuMsg.angular_velocity()/linear_acceleration() straight into the
           JSON "gyro"/"accel_body" fields with ZERO rotation applied:
           modelXYZToAirplaneXForwardZDown (the FLU->FRD body-frame
           transform) is composed and used only for position, velocity and
           quaternion in this plugin version, never for the IMU message.
           With a zero-rotation <pose>, the roll-axis gyro reads correctly
           (FLU +X = FRD +X, no flip needed) but pitch and yaw show no sign
           flip at all, so raw FLU data reaches ArduPlane's FRD-assuming
           AHRS/EKF unconverted - the root cause of a persistent
           "AHRS: DCM Roll/Pitch inconsistent" prearm failure.

           CORRECTION: since ArduPilotPlugin never rotates the IMU message
           itself, the transform is baked into THIS sensor's own mounting
           ROTATION instead (position offset unchanged): a 180 deg rotation
           about body X, numerically identical to
           modelXYZToAirplaneXForwardZDown's own compiled default
           (Pose3d(0,0,0,GZ_PI,0,0)) and to plugins/sensors/Frames.hh's
           kQFluToFrdRot.

           WHY THIS GENUINELY CHANGES THE SENSOR OUTPUT (not a cosmetic
           mounting label):
           - gz-sim Physics.cc: for a non-link entity such as a sensor with
             a <pose> offset from its parent link, the reported
             components::AngularVelocity/LinearAcceleration are computed as
             entityWorldPose.Rot().RotateVectorReverse(entityWorldVec) - the
             world-frame vector is rotated into the entity's own
             fully-composed world orientation, chaining the parent link's
             rotation with this sensor's local <pose> rotation. A rotation
             here therefore rotates the raw gyro/accel this sensor reports,
             exactly as a physically re-oriented chip would.
           - gz-sensors ImuSensor::Update() rotates gravity into the same
             fully-composed local frame for the accelerometer's
             specific-force contribution, so accel_body gets the same
             rotation consistently, gravity compensation included.
           - Algebra check: RotateVectorReverse by a pure 180 deg-about-X
             rotation R (self-inverse, R = diag(1,-1,-1)) applied to an
             FLU link-frame vector gives (x,-y,-z), exactly FluFrdSwap().
             A pure body +X (roll) rate is left unchanged, matching the
             already-correct roll-axis result; pure +Y (pitch) or +Z (yaw)
             rates flip sign, matching the required FLU->FRD conversion.
           - This sensor's own orientation quaternion output does change as
             a side effect, but is irrelevant here: CreateStateJSON() never
             reads it. ArduPilot's quaternion, position and velocity come
             from a separate wldAToBdyA-transformed pose pipeline, untouched
             by this rotation.

           Scope: this affects ONLY imu_sensor_ardupilot's <pose> rotation.
           imu_sensor above is not touched; its own FLU->FRD conversion,
           where needed, is handled in software via Frames.hh::FluFrdSwap(). -->
      <sensor name="imu_sensor_ardupilot" type="imu">
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>100</update_rate>
        <pose degrees="true">0.168309 0 0.100000 180 0 0</pose>
        <imu>
          <orientation_reference_frame>
            <localization>CUSTOM</localization>
            <custom_rpy parent_frame="world">0 0 0</custom_rpy>
          </orientation_reference_frame>
        </imu>
      </sensor>

      <!-- GPS (navsat): reports lat/lon/alt (WGS84, per world
           spherical_coordinates) and velocity_east/north/up explicitly
           (gz.msgs.NavSat, CONFIRMED via navsat.proto) - i.e. ENU by
           construction, no ambiguity. Position reference origin is the
           world's <spherical_coordinates> block
           (lat=0,lon=0,elevation=0 - PLACEHOLDER_ORIGIN, DATA_REQUIRED for
           Falcon's real flight-test location, see SENSORS.md sec 5). -->
      <sensor name="gps_sensor" type="navsat">
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>5</update_rate>
        <topic>/model/falcon_v2/sensors/gps</topic>
        <pose>0.168309 0 0.100000 0 0 0</pose>
      </sensor>

      <!-- Barometer (air_pressure): publishes gz.msgs.FluidPressure (Pa).
           reference_altitude=0 matches the world spherical_coordinates
           elevation=0 placeholder origin above - consistent, not a second,
           independently-chosen number. ArduPilot's own barometer backend
           converts pressure->altitude itself (out of scope here, next
           stage). -->
      <sensor name="baro_sensor" type="air_pressure">
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>50</update_rate>
        <topic>/model/falcon_v2/sensors/baro</topic>
        <pose>0.168309 0 0.100000 0 0 0</pose>
        <air_pressure>
          <reference_altitude>0</reference_altitude>
        </air_pressure>
      </sensor>

      <!-- Magnetometer: deliberately NOT declared here as a native
           <sensor type="magnetometer">. On this Gazebo Sim Harmonic
           installation, the native sensor plus world-level
           gz-sim-magnetometer-system pairing publishes a constant
           ~0.32-magnitude field_tesla output that is completely
           unresponsive to a 100x change in the declared world
           <magnetic_field> value - a reproducible defect in the installed
           libraries, not a sign, frame or config error in this model.
           See the plugins/sensors/MagnetometerSystem.hh header comment for
           the full evidence trail.

           It is replaced by the custom FalconV2Magnetometer plugin (a
           model-level <plugin> block near FalconV2Pitot below), which
           publishes on the SAME topic (/model/falcon_v2/sensors/mag,
           gz.msgs.Magnetometer, field_tesla, body FLU frame, zero relative
           rotation to base_link) so no downstream consumer needs to change,
           and which genuinely consumes a documented Earth-field value. -->
    </link>

    <!-- ============================================================
         LEFT AILERON: movable link, mesh-pose strategy (B): link origin
